<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Godot-free unit + fuzz suite for the pure networking foundation (scripts/net/).

    Referencing mechanism for Godot types:
    The foundation classes under test (NetCodec, SnapshotBuffer, MoveState, ...) use
    Godot value types such as Godot.Vector3 and the pure static math in Godot.Mathf.
    Those live in GodotSharp.dll, an ordinary managed assembly with NO native engine
    dependency for the struct math and codec paths these tests exercise, so they run
    without a Godot editor or runtime present.

    We take a ProjectReference on the game's SuperFindGreatDeal.csproj. That does three
    things in one line: (1) builds the game so its output is fresh, (2) exposes every public
    foundation type, and (3) flows GodotSharp.dll in transitively as a runtime dependency.
    So SuperFindGreatDeal.dll and GodotSharp.dll both land next to the test assembly.

    (This project keeps its SailNet.Tests name and RootNamespace: the rename pass at the fork
    covered the game project and solution only, because a test-assembly rename is a namespace
    change and namespaces are deliberately not being swept yet. See docs/PRUNE-BACKLOG.md.)

    The game project is a Godot.NET.Sdk project; a plain Microsoft.NET.Sdk project can
    still reference it because the produced MpFoundation.dll / GodotSharp.dll are normal
    .NET 8 managed assemblies. No Godot editor, runtime, or ProjectSettings are touched
    by anything these tests call (all targets are static/pure or plain data structs).

    Scope / one skipped test:
    Every target parses/round-trips without a live engine, so nothing was skipped for
    engine reasons. There is ONE [Fact(Skip=...)]:
    ServerInputQueueTests.SeqApproachingUintMax_InOrderStream_DrainsInsteadOfWedging.
    It encodes the correct non-wedge invariant for the uint sequence boundary, but the
    overflow-safe guard it needs (`seq - _lastConsumed > MaxSeqLead`) is NOT present in
    this branch: ServerInputQueue still uses `seq > _lastConsumed + MaxSeqLead`, whose
    addition overflows near uint.MaxValue. The current (buggy) behavior is pinned green by
    the companion test SeqNearUintMax_CurrentBranch_WedgesQueue_RegressionMarker. When the
    guard lands, that marker goes red: delete it and un-skip the invariant test. (Fixing
    ServerInputQueue is out of scope here — tests are additive only.)
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>

    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>

    <RootNamespace>SailNet.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.6.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.4.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.4.5">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.0">
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\SuperFindGreatDeal.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
